  },
  {
    "content": "Run frontend tests/build checks to confirm no behavior regressions",
    "status": "completed",
    "priority": "high"
  }
]

Things to fix

1. Dark ΓÇ£barsΓÇ¥ that look like broken UI (main issue)
Across modules, output is shown in <pre class="log"> elements. The stylesheet gives them a dark terminal-style background even when they are empty:

.log {
  margin: 10px 0 0;
  max-height: 230px;
  overflow: auto;
  border-radius: 12px;
  border: 1px solid #2c4050;
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, #13202b 0%, #101922 100%);
  color: #d7f2f7;
  padding: 10px;
  font-family: "IBM Plex Mono", "Consolas", monospace;
  font-size: 0.81rem;
  line-height: 1.42;
}
Those blocks are always in the DOM (e.g. hiring job log, support attach log):

		card("Job Posting", field("job-code", "Code")+field("job-title", "Title")+fieldArea("job-description", "Description", 3)+`<div class="actions-row"><button class="btn" id="hiring-create-job" type="button">Create Job</button><button class="ghost-btn" id="hiring-load-jobs" type="button">Load Jobs</button></div><pre class="log" id="hiring-jobs-log"></pre><div id="hiring-job-msg" class="muted" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>`, ``) +
So before any action runs, you see a wide dark strip under buttonsΓÇöit reads as an empty progress bar or layout bug, not as ΓÇ£log output (empty).ΓÇ¥ The same pattern repeats on support attachments and elsewhere.

2. Login layout and polish
On /, the two panels are not aligned (different vertical position/heights), shadow treatment differs (strong shadow on the form card, none on the hero), and muted helper text / input borders are quite lightΓÇölikely weak contrast for WCAG. The primary Sign In width also does not line up cleanly with the fields.

3. Dashboard
Status tiles repeat the label in the value (e.g. label ΓÇ£UserΓÇ¥ and body ΓÇ£User: adminΓÇ¥), which feels noisy.
Top bar: Logout does not align visually with the module pills.
Module cards: in a typical viewport, bottom content can feel clipped relative to the scroll area (tight vertical rhythm).
4. Dense forms (hiring / support)
Long pages of stacked operational forms read more like internal tools than a guided flow; that may be intentional for an MVP, but it affects scanability.
Minor misalignments (e.g. paired buttons not the same height; chips close to card edges).
5. Accessibility tree structure
Browser snapshots show nav, headings, and fields largely as siblings under the document, with headings not cleanly wrapping their sections. That often means landmarks / grouping (<section aria-labelledby="ΓÇª">, fieldset/legend where appropriate) are weakΓÇöscreen reader navigation will be harder than it needs to be.

